mode: "100644"
text: "# Faceless Shorts: style selection\n\nThis map summarizes the six styles named by the supplied faceless-shorts package. Each package\nstyle has a separate STYLE.md, example project, and style sheet. Read the selected STYLE.md and\ninspect its example before authoring shots; this summary does not replace those files.\n\n| Style ID | Best fit from the supplied guide | Constraints to preserve |\n|---|---|---|\n| deep-dive | Stylized 3D body and science explainers | Explain causes with visible cutaways; check medical claims and avoid graphic or alarming invention. |\n| night-file | Mystery and true-crime stories | Separate established facts from theories; avoid presenting speculation as a solved case. |\n| inside-out | Flat 2D mechanisms and what-happens-if explanations | Keep diagrams readable and make each visual correspond to a stated mechanism. |\n| stick-story | Survival scenarios and decision steps | Verify advice against authoritative sources; distinguish hypothetical choices from emergency guidance. |\n| paper-atlas | Historical events, routes, and maps | Verify dates and places; paraphrase records rather than inventing quotations. |\n| tall-tale | Funny or surprising true stories | Preserve the factual sequence; do not invent dialogue or evidence to improve a punchline. |\n\nThe source examples describe different palettes, characters, caption treatments, and sound cues.\nTreat those as style-specific implementation constraints, not universal requirements. Do not imitate\na creator's exact shots, scripts, logos, or distinctive character designs. Build original scenes\nfrom high-level visual traits and the user's content.\n\nFor a new style, ask for references and separate visible details from inferred motion. A still image\ndoes not prove how animation behaves.\n"
